<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golestan Palace</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Golestan Palace is a royal complex in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, developed from a Safavid citadel into the chief residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> shahs after Tehran became the capital in the late eighteenth century. Rebuilt and decorated in the nineteenth century, it blends Persian and European styles and holds rich collections, including the photographs of Naser al-Din Shah. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2013.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golestan-palace.jpg" alt="Golestan Palace" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZarlokX · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palais_du_Golestan,_T%C3%A9h%C3%A9ran_(5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
